 "chapter": "4",
 "title": "L’Hôpital’s Rule",
 "short": "L’Hôpital’s Rule",
 "lead": "A limit of a quotient whose numerator and denominator both go to 0, or both to infinity, can often be found from the quotient of their derivatives. This section states that rule, L’Hôpital’s rule, rewrites the other indeterminate forms so that it applies, and uses it to compare how fast functions grow.",
 "objectives": [
  "Recognize when to apply L’Hôpital’s rule.",
  "Identify indeterminate forms produced by quotients, products, subtractions, and powers, and apply L’Hôpital’s rule in each case.",
  "Describe the relative growth rates of functions."
 ],
 "summary_html": "<ul><li><span data-concept=\"lhopitals-rule\">L’Hôpital’s rule</span> can be used to evaluate the limit of a quotient when the <span data-concept=\"indeterminate-forms\">indeterminate form</span> $\\frac{0}{0}$ or $\\infty /\\infty$ arises.</li><li><span data-concept=\"lhopitals-rule\">L’Hôpital’s rule</span> can also be applied to other <span data-concept=\"indeterminate-forms\">indeterminate forms</span> if they can be rewritten in terms of a limit involving a quotient that has the <span data-concept=\"indeterminate-forms\">indeterminate form</span> $\\frac{0}{0}$ or $\\infty /\\infty.$</li><li>The <span data-concept=\"exponential-function\">exponential function</span> ${e}^{x}$ <span data-concept=\"growth-rates-of-functions\">grows faster</span> than any <span data-concept=\"power-function\">power function</span> ${x}^{p},$ $p\\gt 0.$</li><li>The <span data-concept=\"logarithmic-function\">logarithmic function</span> $\\ln x$ <span data-concept=\"growth-rates-of-functions\">grows more slowly</span> than any <span data-concept=\"power-function\">power function</span> ${x}^{p},$ $p\\gt 0.$</li></ul><section id=\"\"><h2></h2>",
