   "prompt": "<p>For the following exercises, determine whether you can apply L’Hôpital’s rule directly. Explain why or why not. Then, indicate if there is some way you can alter the limit so you can apply L’Hôpital’s rule.</p><p>$\\lim_{x\\to 0}{x}^{2/x}$</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "Not directly: $x^{2/x}$ is a power, not a quotient. Taking logarithms, $\\ln y=\\frac{2}{x}\\ln x=\\frac{2\\ln x}{x}.$ As $x\\to 0^{+},$ $\\ln x\\to-\\infty$ and $\\frac{2}{x}\\to\\infty,$ so $\\ln y\\to-\\infty$ and $y\\to 0:$ the form is $0^{\\infty},$ which is not indeterminate, and the limit from the right is $0$ without L’Hôpital’s rule. (For $x\\lt 0$ the power $x^{2/x}$ is not defined for most $x,$ so the limit is taken from the right.)"
   }
